{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rust","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:11.363Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/rust/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/rust/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Rust image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Rust?\n\nRust is a systems programming language sponsored by Mozilla Research. It is designed to be a \"safe, concurrent, practical language\", supporting functional and imperative-procedural paradigms. Rust is syntactically similar to C++, but is designed for better memory safety while maintaining performance.\n\nSee: [Rust (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rust_%28programming_language%29)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `1`\n- `1-cm2.0`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of the image. Additional tags that are specific to rust version, Mariner build, or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running a simple Rust app\n\n`1.` Create a simple Dockerfile to generate a new image that includes the necessary content.\n\n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rust:1\n\n    WORKDIR /usr/src/app\n\n    # Copy your rust app source code files to the work directory.\n    COPY my-rust-app/ .\n\n    # Run cargo install to build the rust app.\n    RUN cargo install --path .\n\n    CMD [ \"myapp\" ]\n\n`2.` Build and run your container image.\n\n    docker build -t rust-app-image .\n    docker run -it --rm --name running-rust-app rust-app-image\n\nThe default container startup command is `bash`.\n\n### Compile your app inside the docker container\n\nThere may be occasions where it is not appropriate to run your app inside a container. To compile, but not run your app inside the Docker instance, you can write something like:\n\n    docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/usr/src/myapp -w /usr/src/myapp mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rust:1 cargo build –release\n\nThis will add your current directory as a volume to the container, set the working directory to the volume, and run the command `cargo build --release`. This tells Cargo (Rust's build system) to compile the crate in *myapp* and output the executable to *target/release/myapp*.\n\n### Launch Mariner shell\n\nThe Rust image ships with a bash shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/rust:1\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslega